---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { LABS, TOTAL_VALUATION_USDM } from '../data/labs';
import { money } from '../lib/format';

const description =
  'What counts as a neolab, how this map encodes valuation, lineage, geography, and research area, and what the data does and does not claim.';
---

<Layout title="About neolabs.fyi" description={description} path="/about" hideFooter>
  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What's a neolab?</h2>
    <p>
      An AI research lab founded by elite researchers from major AI labs, professors, or
      post-economic entrepreneurs. They're typically pre-product-market-fit and sub-revenue-scale,
      yet raise at unicorn-plus valuations to chase long-term breakthroughs.
    </p>
    <p>
      That definition deliberately excludes the incumbents (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google DeepMind,
      xAI), non-profits, and companies that are really at revenue scale — Mistral, Cohere, Runway,
      Luma, Black Forest Labs — along with pure hardware companies. Chinese labs <em>are</em>{' '}
      included: leaving them out would make this a map of American AI rather than of the field.
      Two of them have since listed in Hong Kong and are marked as such, since a market cap that
      moves daily is not the same kind of number as a private round.
    </p>

    <h2>What you're looking at</h2>
    <p>
      {LABS.length} labs, roughly {money(TOTAL_VALUATION_USDM)} in combined paper valuation, in four
      views of the same canvas:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Valuation</strong> — a single pack, with bubble <em>area</em> proportional to
        valuation, so a $100B lab really is ~300× the area of a $335M one. A log scale would have
        flattened exactly the disparity worth seeing. Founding year lives in the timeline filter
        rather than on an axis, because only a handful of these labs predate 2022.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lineage</strong> — each lab wired to the organisations its founders came out of.
        This is the actual story of the list: researchers leaving OpenAI, DeepMind, Meta, and
        Google and raising billions.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Geography</strong> — where the labs actually are.</li>
      <li><strong>Research area</strong> — clustered by what they work on.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>On colour</h2>
    <p>
      Colour encodes valuation, not research area. There are more than a dozen research areas here,
      and no categorical palette can keep that many hues reliably distinguishable — including for
      the roughly 1 in 12 men with colour vision deficiency. Research area is carried by position,
      labels, and the tooltip instead; colour does the one job it's unambiguously good at, which is
      magnitude.
    </p>

    <h2>Caveats</h2>
    <p>
      Valuations in this market are messy, and the site keeps that messiness rather than hiding it.
      A figure shown as <code>&gt;$1B</code> is a lower bound, <code>~$4B</code> is approximate, and
      anything marked rumored is exactly that. Headquarters and founder backgrounds are compiled
      from public reporting; where a lab is genuinely split across two cities, the primary office is
      used.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
